  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 138 PM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nWith drier air working into the region and weakening gradient winds, \nThursday night lows in the 20s are expected across the higher terrain, \nwith lows in the 30s across the plains, leading to the potential for \nfrost and the first freeze of the season across southeast Colorado. \nWill keep Freeze Watch in tact for northern portions of the plains for \nThursday night, and may need to add areas to it, as latest model data\nis slightly cooler across southern portions of the southeast plains. \nMay need to add another Freeze Watch/Warning for Friday night as well\nacross the plains, with latest data indicating the end of the growing \nseason becoming more likely across southern Colorado. \n\n\nOtherwise, warmer and drier conditions remain in the offing for the weekend \ninto early next week, as upper level ridging builds into the region from \nthe west. This gives way to increasing southwest flow aloft ahead of another \nsystem moving across the Great Basin bringing increasing chances of \nprecipitation to areas along and west of the ContDvd through the middle \nof next week, with models differing on the timing and location of said\nsystem lifting out across the Rockies through the end of next week.",
